## Public mint limit bypass via post-callback counter update in `mint` of `NextGenCore`

[smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol#L187-L200](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol#L187-L200)

```solidity
    // mint called from minterContract

    function mint(uint256 mintIndex, address _mintingAddress , address _mintTo, string memory _tokenData, uint256 _saltfun_o, uint256 _collectionID, uint256 phase) external {
        require(msg.sender == minterContract, "Caller is not the Minter Contract");
        collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply = collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply + 1;
        if (collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionTotalSupply >= collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply) {
            _mintProcessing(mintIndex, _mintTo, _tokenData, _collectionID, _saltfun_o);
            if (phase == 1) {
                tokensMintedAllowlistAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] = tokensMintedAllowlistAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] + 1;
            } else {
                tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] = tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] + 1;
            }
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`NextGenCore.mint` updates `tokensMintedPerAddress` only after mint processing completes. Because mint processing uses ERC721 safe minting, a contract recipient can reenter through `onERC721Received` before the increment of `tokensMintedPerAddress` in the public phase branch (`phase != 1`) of `NextGenCore.mint`, causing MinterContract limit checks to read stale mint counters and allowing excess public mints.

### Technical Details

The root cause is the public-sale counter update via increment of `tokensMintedPerAddress` in the public phase branch (`phase != 1`) of `NextGenCore.mint` being executed after `_mintProcessing` has already completed. `_mintProcessing` performs `_safeMint`, which invokes `onERC721Received` when `_mintTo` is a contract.

During this receiver callback, the recipient contract can call back into the registered `MinterContract` and initiate another mint for the same collection and same minting address. Minter-side public mint limits are enforced by reading `NextGenCore.retrieveTokensMintedPublicPerAddress`, which returns `tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress]`.

Because the counter increment in the public phase branch of `NextGenCore.mint` has not happened yet for the in-progress mint, the nested mint observes the old counter value. The attacker can recursively repeat this process, causing each nested mint to pass the same stale per-address limit check. Only after the nested calls unwind does the increment of `tokensMintedPerAddress` in the public phase branch of `NextGenCore.mint` execute once per mint, at which point the limit has already been bypassed.

The vulnerable path is the `phase != 1` branch in `NextGenCore.mint`, where public-sale mint accounting is performed after an external callback-capable mint interaction instead of before it.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify this issue statically by checking:

- `NextGenCore.mint` calls `_mintProcessing` before reaching the increment of `tokensMintedPerAddress` in the public phase branch (`phase != 1`).
- `_mintProcessing` eventually calls `_safeMint(_recipient, _mintIndex)`.
- `_safeMint` invokes `IERC721Receiver.onERC721Received` when `_mintTo` is a contract.
- `tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress]` is incremented only after that receiver callback returns.
- `MinterContract` validates public mint limits using `retrieveTokensMintedPublicPerAddress` before calling `NextGenCore.mint`.
- There is no effective `nonReentrant` guard preventing reentry from the ERC721 receiver callback back into the public mint flow.
- Public minting allows a contract to be the caller and/or token recipient so that `onERC721Received` can execute attacker-controlled logic.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- A public mint phase is active for the target collection.
- The collection has a finite `maxCollectionPurchases` value enforced by `MinterContract`.
- The attacker can mint to a contract implementing `IERC721Receiver`.
- The attacker contract initiates the mint so the tracked `_mintingAddress` corresponds to the reentering contract.
- The attacker provides sufficient ETH for every nested mint.
- `MinterContract` relies on `NextGenCore.retrieveTokensMintedPublicPerAddress` for public per-address mint-limit enforcement and does not independently reserve the mint count before calling `NextGenCore.mint`.

### Proof of Concept

1. Assume a collection has `maxCollectionPurchases = 1` and public minting is active.
2. The attacker deploys a contract implementing `onERC721Received`.
3. The attacker contract calls the public mint function on `MinterContract` to mint one token to itself.
4. `MinterContract` checks `retrieveTokensMintedPublicPerAddress(collectionId, attackerContract) == 0`, so the mint is allowed.
5. `MinterContract` calls `NextGenCore.mint(..., _mintingAddress = attackerContract, _mintTo = attackerContract, phase != 1)`.
6. `NextGenCore.mint` reaches `_mintProcessing`, which performs `_safeMint`.
7. `_safeMint` calls `attackerContract.onERC721Received`.
8. Inside `onERC721Received`, the attacker contract calls the public mint function again.
9. The nested `MinterContract` call again reads `retrieveTokensMintedPublicPerAddress(...) == 0` because the increment of `tokensMintedPerAddress` in the public phase branch of the outer `NextGenCore.mint` has not executed yet.
10. The nested mint succeeds. This can be repeated recursively until gas, ETH, or collection supply is exhausted.
11. After callbacks unwind, the increment of `tokensMintedPerAddress` in the public phase branch of `NextGenCore.mint` increments the counter for each mint, but the attacker has already minted more than the configured per-address limit.

### Remediation

Apply checks-effects-interactions: update the public mint counter before any callback-capable mint processing occurs. If `_mintProcessing` later reverts, the counter update is reverted as part of the same transaction.

Corrected ordering:

```solidity
} else {
tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] = tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] + 1;
}
_mintProcessing(mintIndex, _mintTo, _tokenData, _collectionID, _saltfun_o);
}
```

Additionally, consider adding a reentrancy guard to the external mint entrypoints in `MinterContract`, but the primary fix is to perform Core-side accounting before `_safeMint` can trigger `onERC721Received`.

## Delayed source-token burn after callback-capable mint in `burnToMint` of `NextGenCore`

[smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol#L211-L223](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol#L211-L223)

```solidity
    // burn to mint called from minterContract

    function burnToMint(uint256 mintIndex, uint256 _burnCollectionID, uint256 _tokenId, uint256 _mintCollectionID, uint256 _saltfun_o, address burner) external {
        require(msg.sender == minterContract, "Caller is not the Minter Contract");
        require(_isApprovedOrOwner(burner, _tokenId), "ERC721: caller is not token owner or approved");
        collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply = collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply + 1;
        if (collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionTotalSupply >= collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply) {
            _mintProcessing(mintIndex, ownerOf(_tokenId), tokenData[_tokenId], _mintCollectionID, _saltfun_o);
            // burn token
            _burn(_tokenId);
            burnAmount[_burnCollectionID] = burnAmount[_burnCollectionID] + 1;
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`NextGenCore.burnToMint` burns the source NFT only after minting the replacement token. Because replacement minting can trigger an ERC721 receiver callback, a malicious source-token owner can sell or transfer the still-unburned source NFT before it is burned from the new owner.

### Technical Details

The root cause is the ordering of the burn in `NextGenCore.burnToMint`: the source token is consumed at the `_burn(_tokenId)` call only after `_mintProcessing(...)` has already executed.

`_mintProcessing` ultimately performs `_safeMint` to `ownerOf(_tokenId)`. If that owner is a contract, `_safeMint` invokes `onERC721Received` on the recipient before returning. During that callback, the burn source token still exists and is still owned by the attacker-controlled contract because the `_burn(_tokenId)` call has not executed yet.

The attacker can use this callback window to transfer or sell the source NFT, for example by accepting a marketplace bid. After the callback returns, execution resumes and the `_burn(_tokenId)` call executes. OpenZeppelin-style internal `_burn` burns the token from whoever owns it at that moment; it does not verify that the current owner is still the original burner or that the new owner authorized the burn.

As a result, the attacker can receive the newly minted burn-to-mint NFT and also sell the source NFT during the same transaction, while the buyer or marketplace receives a token that is immediately burned before the transaction completes.

### Verification Checklist

- Check that `NextGenCore.burnToMint` calls `_mintProcessing(...)` before executing `_burn(_tokenId)`.
- Check that `_mintProcessing(...)` performs a callback-capable ERC721 safe mint to the current owner of the source token.
- Check that the source token remains transferable during the receiver callback because the `_burn(_tokenId)` call has not executed yet.
- Check that ERC721 `transferFrom` / `safeTransferFrom` can be called by the attacker-controlled source-token owner during `onERC721Received`.
- Check that internal `_burn(_tokenId)` burns the token from its current owner and does not re-check that the current owner is the original burner.
- Check that there is no token lock or transfer restriction preventing the source token from being transferred between the authorization check and the `_burn(_tokenId)` call.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- Burn-to-mint is enabled for the relevant burn collection and mint collection.
- The attacker owns an eligible burn-source NFT through a contract implementing `IERC721Receiver`.
- The attacker can successfully call the protocol’s burn-to-mint flow through the registered minter.
- The mint collection has remaining supply, so `_mintProcessing(...)` executes.
- A buyer, marketplace bid, or other receiving address/contract accepts the source NFT during the callback window.
- Admin and protocol configuration are honest and otherwise correct.

### Proof of Concept

1. The attacker deploys a contract implementing `onERC721Received`.
2. The attacker-controlled contract owns an eligible source NFT for a configured burn-to-mint collection pair.
3. A marketplace has an active bid for that source NFT, or another victim is willing to receive it.
4. The attacker contract calls the burn-to-mint function on the registered minter.
5. The minter calls `NextGenCore.burnToMint(...)`.
6. `NextGenCore.burnToMint` validates the burn authorization and enters `_mintProcessing(...)` before burning the source token.
7. `_mintProcessing(...)` safe-mints the replacement NFT to the attacker contract and invokes `onERC721Received`.
8. Inside `onERC721Received`, the attacker contract transfers or sells the original source NFT to the marketplace/buyer and receives payment.
9. The callback returns.
10. Execution resumes in `NextGenCore.burnToMint`, and the `_burn(_tokenId)` call executes.
11. The source NFT is burned from the marketplace/buyer, while the attacker keeps the replacement NFT and the sale proceeds.

### Remediation

Apply checks-effects-interactions in `burnToMint`: verify mint capacity, cache the original recipient and token data, burn the source token before any callback-capable minting, then mint the replacement token.

```solidity
function burnToMint(uint256 mintIndex, uint256 _burnCollectionID, uint256 _tokenId, uint256 _mintCollectionID, uint256 _saltfun_o, address burner) external {
require(msg.sender == minterContract, "Caller is not the Minter Contract");
require(_isApprovedOrOwner(burner, _tokenId), "ERC721: caller is not token owner or approved");
uint256 nextCirculationSupply = collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply + 1;
require(collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionTotalSupply >= nextCirculationSupply, "Max supply reached");
address recipient = ownerOf(_tokenId);
string memory sourceTokenData = tokenData[_tokenId];
_burn(_tokenId);
burnAmount[_burnCollectionID] = burnAmount[_burnCollectionID] + 1;
collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply = nextCirculationSupply;
_mintProcessing(mintIndex, recipient, sourceTokenData, _mintCollectionID, _saltfun_o);
}
```

A token-locking mechanism that prevents transfers of `_tokenId` during burn-to-mint would also mitigate the issue, but burning before minting is the preferred fix.

## Premature Auction Finalization Before Settlement Completion in `claimAuction` of `auctionDemo`

[hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120)

```solidity
    // claim Token After Auction

    function claimAuction(uint256 _tokenid) public WinnerOrAdminRequired(_tokenid,this.claimAuction.selector){
        require(block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
        auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
        uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);
        address ownerOfToken = IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid);
        address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);
        for (uint256 i=0; i< auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i ++) {
            if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == highestBidder && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid == highestBid && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
                (bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
                emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
                emit Refund(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else {}
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`auctionDemo.claimAuction` marks an auction as claimed before NFT transfer and ETH settlement are completed. If any downstream settlement payment fails without reverting, the auction remains finalized, preventing retries and potentially locking bidder refunds or seller proceeds permanently.

### Technical Details

In `claimAuction`, the auction is finalized by the assignment `auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true`:

```solidity
auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
```

This state change occurs before the function completes settlement: transferring the NFT to the winner, sending the winning bid to `owner()`, and refunding losing bidders.

The issue is that the later ETH settlement operations use low-level calls and only emit the returned `success` flag. They do not revert or restore retryable state when a payment fails. Therefore, if a recipient rejects ETH, lacks a payable fallback, consumes excessive gas, or otherwise causes the low-level call to return `false`, the auction remains marked as claimed.

Because `auctionClaim[_tokenid]` is then permanently `true`, the require precondition checking `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false` prevents any future call to `claimAuction` for the same token. The failed settlement cannot be retried through this function, and affected ETH remains trapped in the contract.

The root cause is premature finality: the contract records the auction as irreversibly claimed before confirming that settlement has completed successfully.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify the issue statically by checking the following:

- Confirm that `claimAuction(uint256)` is the only highlighted function responsible for finalizing auction settlement.
- Confirm that the require precondition checking `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false` blocks future claims once `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == true`.
- Confirm that the assignment `auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true` sets the state before settlement actions complete.
- Inspect the subsequent settlement logic and confirm that ETH transfers are performed using low-level `.call`.
- Confirm that the boolean result of those calls is not required to be `true`.
- Confirm that there is no later mechanism in the highlighted section to reset `auctionClaim[_tokenid]` if settlement partially fails.
- Confirm that bidders cannot cancel bids after the auction end time, so failed post-auction refunds cannot be recovered through cancellation.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The auction has ended.
- `minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid)` returns `true`.
- `auctionClaim[_tokenid]` is initially `false`.
- At least one settlement recipient is unable or unwilling to receive ETH through the low-level call.
- The failed ETH transfer does not revert the full transaction because the return value is ignored.
- No separate administrative recovery mechanism exists to retry or recover failed auction settlement funds.

### Proof of Concept

1. A token auction is active.
2. A bidder participates from a contract address whose `receive()` or `fallback()` function always reverts.
3. Another bidder places a higher valid bid and becomes the auction winner.
4. The auction reaches its end time.
5. The winner or an authorized admin calls `claimAuction(_tokenid)`.
6. At the assignment `auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true`, the contract sets:

```solidity
auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
```

7. The NFT transfer and winning bid settlement proceed.
8. When the contract attempts to refund the losing reverting bidder, the low-level ETH call returns `false`.
9. The transaction does not revert.
10. The refund remains in the contract.
11. Any later attempt to call `claimAuction(_tokenid)` reverts at the require precondition checking `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false` because `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == true`.
12. The failed refund cannot be retried through the auction claim flow.

### Remediation

Record claim finality only after settlement has completed successfully. The preferred fix is to require all settlement calls to succeed and move the `auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true` assignment to the end of the settlement process.

```solidity
// Move this assignment to after all settlement transfers have succeeded.
```

A complete fix should also require successful ETH settlement before finalizing the auction:

```solidity
(bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
require(success, "Owner payment failed");
```

```solidity
(bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
require(success, "Refund failed");
```

```solidity
auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
```

## Reentrant ERC721 receiver callback can refund the winning bid during `claimAuction` in `auctionDemo`

[hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120)

```solidity
    // claim Token After Auction

    function claimAuction(uint256 _tokenid) public WinnerOrAdminRequired(_tokenid,this.claimAuction.selector){
        require(block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
        auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
        uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);
        address ownerOfToken = IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid);
        address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);
        for (uint256 i=0; i< auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i ++) {
            if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == highestBidder && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid == highestBid && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
                (bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
                emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
                emit Refund(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else {}
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`auctionDemo.claimAuction` transfers the NFT to the highest bidder via `safeTransferFrom` before invalidating the winning bid or completing ETH settlement. A contract bidder can reenter during `onERC721Received`, withdraw its still-active bid, and finalize ownership without paying.

### Technical Details

In `claimAuction`, the winning bid is detected by the condition matching the `highestBidder`, `highestBid` amount, and active `status` in the settlement loop, but the bid remains active while the NFT is transferred via `IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom` immediately after.

`IERC721.safeTransferFrom` is an external call. If `highestBidder` is a contract, the ERC721 implementation invokes `onERC721Received` on that contract before `claimAuction` continues to the ETH payment to `owner()`.

At this point:

- the winning bid entry still has `status == true`;
- the NFT has already been transferred, or is in the middle of being safely transferred, to the bidder;
- ETH settlement has not yet been completed;
- `claimAuction` only set the auction claim flag, but bid cancellation logic is not protected by that flag;
- the auction end boundary allows `claimAuction` at `block.timestamp >= endTime`, while cancellation is allowed at `block.timestamp <= endTime`.

Therefore, when the claim transaction is mined at exactly `endTime`, the malicious highest bidder’s `onERC721Received` callback can call `cancelBid` for its winning bid. That cancellation sees the bid as active and refunds the bid amount back to the bidder.

After the callback returns, execution resumes at the ETH payment to `owner()` using the same `highestBid` amount. If the winning bid was already withdrawn, the payment can fail due to insufficient balance. Because the `success` value is only emitted and not required, the transaction still completes. The attacker keeps the NFT and recovers the winning bid.

The root cause is the unsafe settlement ordering in the `safeTransferFrom` call to `highestBidder`: an untrusted ERC721 receiver callback is invoked before the winning bid is finalized and before ETH payment is made atomic with NFT delivery.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify this issue statically by checking:

- The `safeTransferFrom` call to `highestBidder` in `claimAuction` invokes `IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(...)`, which may be an attacker-controlled contract.
- No code before the `safeTransferFrom` call marks the winning `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i]` entry as inactive or settled.
- `safeTransferFrom` to a contract recipient invokes `onERC721Received`, creating an external callback before the subsequent ETH payment to `owner()`.
- `cancelBid` allows cancellation while `block.timestamp <= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.
- `claimAuction` allows settlement while `block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.
- `cancelBid` does not reject cancellation when `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == true`.
- The ETH payment call to `owner()` does not require `success == true`, so failed ETH payment does not revert the already executed settlement flow.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The highest bidder is an attacker-controlled smart contract implementing `onERC721Received`.
- The attacker placed the winning bid through that contract.
- `auctionDemo` is approved to transfer the auctioned NFT from `ownerOfToken`.
- The claim transaction is included with `block.timestamp == minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.
- The attacker contract knows the index of its winning bid in `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]`.
- No external protocol mechanism prevents `cancelBid` from being called during the ERC721 receiver callback.

### Proof of Concept

1. Attacker deploys a bidder contract that:
   - calls `participateToAuction` with ETH;
   - stores the bid index;
   - implements `onERC721Received`;
   - calls `auctionDemo.cancelBid(tokenId, bidIndex)` inside `onERC721Received`.

2. The attacker places the highest bid from the malicious contract.

3. At exactly `minter.getAuctionEndTime(tokenId)`, the attacker calls `claimAuction(tokenId)` from the malicious bidder contract.

4. `claimAuction` enters the settlement loop and matches the winning bid via the condition check on `bidder`, `bid`, and `status`.

5. The `safeTransferFrom` call transfers the NFT to the malicious bidder contract.

6. The ERC721 contract invokes `onERC721Received` on the malicious bidder.

7. Inside the callback, the malicious bidder calls `cancelBid(tokenId, bidIndex)`.

8. `cancelBid` succeeds because:
   - the bid is still active;
   - the bidder is `msg.sender`;
   - `block.timestamp <= endTime` is true at the exact boundary.

9. The malicious bidder receives back its winning bid.

10. Execution returns to `claimAuction`.

11. The ETH payment to `owner()` attempts to pay the winning bid amount, but the relevant ETH has already been withdrawn. The call can return `false`.

12. Because `success` is not required, `claimAuction` completes.

13. Final state:
   - attacker owns the NFT;
   - attacker recovered the winning bid;
   - seller/protocol may not receive payment;
   - `auctionClaim[tokenId]` remains finalized.

### Remediation

Apply checks-effects-interactions: finalize the winning bid before invoking the ERC721 receiver callback, and require ETH payment success so NFT delivery cannot complete without settlement.

```solidity
for (uint256 i=0; i< auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i ++) {
if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == highestBidder && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid == highestBid && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status = false;
(bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
require(success, "Owner payment failed");
IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
} else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status = false;
(bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
emit Refund(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
}
}
```

## Inclusive Auction End Boundary Enables Reentrant Winning Bid Cancellation in `claimAuction` of `auctionDemo`

[hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120)

```solidity
    // claim Token After Auction

    function claimAuction(uint256 _tokenid) public WinnerOrAdminRequired(_tokenid,this.claimAuction.selector){
        require(block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
        auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
        uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);
        address ownerOfToken = IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid);
        address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);
        for (uint256 i=0; i< auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i ++) {
            if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == highestBidder && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid == highestBid && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
                (bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
                emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
                emit Refund(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else {}
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`auctionDemo.claimAuction` allows settlement at `block.timestamp == auctionEndTime`, while bid cancellation remains allowed through the same timestamp. A contract winner can reenter during NFT receipt, cancel its winning bid, recover ETH, and still receive the NFT.

### Technical Details

The root cause is the inclusive auction-end check in `claimAuction`:

```solidity
require(block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
```

This permits auction claiming when `block.timestamp` is exactly equal to `minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`. In the surrounding auction logic, bid cancellation is also permitted at the same timestamp because cancellation checks use `block.timestamp <= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.

As a result, there is a boundary timestamp where both settlement and cancellation are valid simultaneously.

During `claimAuction`, the contract snapshots the winning price and winner:

```solidity
uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);
address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);
```

The settlement flow then transfers the NFT to `highestBidder` using `safeTransferFrom`. If the winner is a contract, its `onERC721Received` hook executes during the transfer. At the exact auction end timestamp, that hook can reenter `auctionDemo` and call `cancelBid` for the winning bid because cancellation is still time-valid.

The attacker’s winning bid is marked inactive and refunded during the callback, but `claimAuction` continues using the stale `highestBid` and `highestBidder` values captured before reentrancy. With only the attacker’s bid present, the contract balance is depleted before the seller payment is attempted, while the NFT transfer has already completed. The attacker receives the NFT and recovers the winning bid.

This is an auction time-boundary inconsistency: the protocol does not enforce a disjoint transition between the bidding/cancellation phase and the settlement phase.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer should verify the following statically:

- `claimAuction` allows execution when `block.timestamp == minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)` in the require statement checking the auction end time, claim status, and auction status at the beginning of `claimAuction`.
- Bid cancellation functions allow execution when `block.timestamp == minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.
- `claimAuction` snapshots `highestBid` and `highestBidder` before settlement in the assignments `uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);` and `address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);` before the settlement loop.
- The settlement flow performs an ERC721 `safeTransferFrom` to `highestBidder`, which can invoke `onERC721Received` on a contract winner.
- Cancellation logic does not check `auctionClaim[_tokenid]`, so setting `auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true` in the statement immediately following the initial require in `claimAuction` does not block reentrant cancellation.
- There is no reentrancy guard preventing the winner’s ERC721 receiver hook from calling back into bid cancellation.
- ETH payment success during settlement is not enforced by a revert, so depleted balance after reentrant cancellation does not roll back the NFT transfer.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The attacker is the highest active bidder for `_tokenid`.
- The attacker bids from a smart contract implementing `onERC721Received`.
- The attacker knows the storage index of its winning bid.
- The `claimAuction` transaction is included in a block where `block.timestamp == minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.
- `minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid)` returns `true`.
- The auction contract is authorized to transfer the NFT from `ownerOfToken`.
- The ERC721 implementation follows standard `safeTransferFrom` behavior and invokes the recipient hook for contract recipients.

### Proof of Concept

1. Attacker deploys a malicious bidder contract with:
   - a payable `receive()`;
   - an `onERC721Received` hook;
   - logic inside `onERC721Received` to call `auctionDemo.cancelBid(_tokenid, winningBidIndex)`.

2. The malicious contract calls `participateToAuction(_tokenid)` with the highest bid.

3. Wait until the block timestamp is exactly equal to `minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.

4. The malicious contract calls `claimAuction(_tokenid)`.

5. In the require statement at the beginning of `claimAuction`, the check passes because `block.timestamp >= auctionEndTime`.

6. Immediately after the require, `auctionClaim[_tokenid]` is set to `true`.

7. `highestBid` is captured via `returnHighestBid(_tokenid)` and `highestBidder` via `returnHighestBidder(_tokenid)`:
   - `highestBid` as the attacker’s bid amount;
   - `highestBidder` as the malicious contract.

8. During settlement, the NFT is transferred with `safeTransferFrom` to the malicious contract.

9. The malicious contract’s `onERC721Received` hook executes before settlement completes.

10. Inside the hook, the malicious contract calls `cancelBid(_tokenid, winningBidIndex)`.

11. Cancellation succeeds because the timestamp is still exactly `auctionEndTime`, satisfying the cancellation-side `<= auctionEndTime` check.

12. The attacker’s winning bid is refunded to the malicious contract.

13. Control returns to `claimAuction`.

14. `claimAuction` continues using the stale `highestBid` and `highestBidder` values captured before the reentrant cancellation.

15. If the attacker was the only bidder, the auction contract no longer has the bid funds needed to pay the seller, while the NFT transfer has already completed.

16. The attacker ends with both:
   - the NFT;
   - the refunded winning bid.

### Remediation

Make the settlement phase start strictly after the cancellation phase. Since cancellation remains valid through `auctionEndTime`, `claimAuction` must require `block.timestamp > auctionEndTime`.

```solidity
require(block.timestamp > minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
```

## Active losing bids are externally refunded before being finalized, enabling reentrant double refunds in `claimAuction` of `auctionDemo`

[hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120)

```solidity
    // claim Token After Auction

    function claimAuction(uint256 _tokenid) public WinnerOrAdminRequired(_tokenid,this.claimAuction.selector){
        require(block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
        auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
        uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);
        address ownerOfToken = IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid);
        address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);
        for (uint256 i=0; i< auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i ++) {
            if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == highestBidder && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid == highestBid && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
                (bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
                emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
                emit Refund(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else {}
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`auctionDemo.claimAuction` refunds losing bidders through an untrusted ETH callback while their bid entry remains active. At the auction end boundary, a malicious losing bidder can reenter cancellation logic and withdraw the same bid again, draining auction settlement funds.

### Technical Details

In `claimAuction`, the settlement loop (for-loop iterating over all entries in `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]`) processes every active bid stored in `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]`.

For losing bids, the low-level call in the else-if branch for active non-winning bids sends ETH directly to `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder` using a low-level call:

```solidity
payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("")
```

However, before this external call:

- the bid’s `status` is still `true`;
- no local settlement lock is applied;
- the bid is not marked as processed;
- the external recipient is fully untrusted and can execute arbitrary callback logic.

As a result, a malicious losing bidder contract can receive the refund and reenter the auction contract before the outer `claimAuction` loop continues. Because the bid is still active during the callback, the bidder can invoke bid-cancellation logic for the same bid at the exact auction end boundary, where claiming and cancellation time predicates overlap.

This causes the same losing bid to be paid twice:

1. once by the refund call in `claimAuction`;
2. once by the reentrant cancellation call while the bid still has `status == true`.

The duplicated refund is paid from the shared auction ETH balance, which includes the winning bid. After the malicious refund drains the balance, the later owner payment (low-level call sending `highestBid` to `owner()`) can fail due to insufficient balance. Because that payment result is only emitted and not enforced, finalization can continue with the NFT transferred while the seller/protocol receives no proceeds.

The root cause is that `claimAuction` performs an external refund interaction before applying the internal effect that makes the bid no longer withdrawable.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify the issue by checking the following:

- Confirm that the settlement loop (for-loop iterating over `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]` in `claimAuction`) iterates over all bid records for a token.
- Confirm that losing active bids enter the else-if branch for active non-winning bids (`else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true)`).
- Confirm that the low-level `call{value: ...}` to the losing bidder in that branch performs an external ETH call to the bidder address.
- Confirm that the bid’s `status` is not changed before the low-level call refunding the losing bidder.
- Confirm that there is no reentrancy guard or settlement-in-progress flag protecting the settlement loop in `claimAuction`.
- Confirm that bid cancellation logic in `cancelBid` and `cancelAllBids` uses the same `status == true` field to authorize withdrawal.
- Confirm that claiming is allowed at `block.timestamp >= auctionEndTime`, while cancellation is allowed at `block.timestamp <= auctionEndTime`, creating an overlap at exactly `auctionEndTime`.
- Confirm that the owner payment result (low-level call sending `highestBid` to `owner()` in the winning bid if-branch) is not required to succeed, so balance depletion does not necessarily revert settlement.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The auction has at least one active losing bid controlled by the attacker through a contract.
- The attacker-controlled losing bidder contract can execute code when receiving ETH.
- The auction is finalized in a block where `block.timestamp == minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid)`.
- The malicious losing bid remains active when `claimAuction` starts.
- The attacker can either control the highest bidder account that calls `claimAuction`, or otherwise induce an authorized highest bidder/admin to finalize at the boundary timestamp.
- The auction contract holds enough ETH from bids for the duplicated refund to consume funds intended for the seller/protocol.
- The NFT transfer from `gencore` is approved and succeeds.

### Proof of Concept

1. Attacker deploys a malicious bidder contract with a payable fallback/receive function.

2. The malicious bidder contract places an initial bid, for example `10 ETH`, becoming the first active bidder.

3. The attacker then places a higher bid from another account or contract, for example `11 ETH`, becoming the current highest bidder.

4. Total ETH held by `auctionDemo` is now `21 ETH`.

5. At exactly `minter.getAuctionEndTime(tokenId)`, the highest bidder calls `claimAuction(tokenId)`.

6. `claimAuction` computes:
   - `highestBid = 11 ETH`;
   - `highestBidder = attacker’s highest-bidder account`.

7. The settlement loop (for-loop iterating over `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]`) reaches the malicious losing bid first.

8. The losing bid branch (else-if for active non-winning bids) executes the low-level call refunding the losing bidder and sends `10 ETH` to the malicious bidder contract.

9. Inside its ETH receive callback, the malicious contract reenters the auction contract and cancels the same bid.

10. The cancellation succeeds because the bid is still marked active and the timestamp is exactly the auction end time.

11. The malicious contract receives another `10 ETH`.

12. Control returns to `claimAuction`.

13. The settlement loop later reaches the winning bid.

14. The NFT is transferred to the highest bidder.

15. The owner payment (low-level call sending `highestBid` to `owner()`) of `11 ETH` fails because only `1 ETH` remains in the contract.

16. Because the payment success value is not required, `claimAuction` completes.

17. Final result:
   - attacker received `20 ETH` back from `21 ETH` deposited;
   - attacker-controlled highest bidder receives the NFT;
   - seller/protocol receives no winning bid payment;
   - auction is marked claimed.

### Remediation

Apply checks-effects-interactions in the settlement loop. Mark each bid as no longer active before making any external ETH call, cache bid data before interaction, and make the seller payment atomic with NFT delivery.

Preferred long-term remediation is to replace push refunds with pull-based withdrawals. As a minimal fix within the settlement loop in `claimAuction`, finalize bid state before external calls and require the owner payment to succeed before NFT delivery.

```solidity
for (uint256 i = 0; i < auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i++) {
auctionInfoStru storage bidInfo = auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i];
if (bidInfo.bidder == highestBidder && bidInfo.bid == highestBid && bidInfo.status == true) {
bidInfo.status = false;
(bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
require(success, "Owner payment failed");
IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
} else if (bidInfo.status == true) {
address bidder = bidInfo.bidder; uint256 bidAmount = bidInfo.bid; bidInfo.status = false; (bool success, ) = payable(bidder).call{value: bidAmount}(""); emit Refund(bidder, _tokenid, success, bidAmount);
}
}
}
```

## Winning auction proceeds are sent to the contract owner instead of the NFT owner in `claimAuction` of `auctionDemo`

[hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2023-10-nextgen/blob/ba73b7d488f7d9655938dc8ec07f095870de46a0/hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol#L102-L120)

```solidity
    // claim Token After Auction

    function claimAuction(uint256 _tokenid) public WinnerOrAdminRequired(_tokenid,this.claimAuction.selector){
        require(block.timestamp >= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid) && auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false && minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true);
        auctionClaim[_tokenid] = true;
        uint256 highestBid = returnHighestBid(_tokenid);
        address ownerOfToken = IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid);
        address highestBidder = returnHighestBidder(_tokenid);
        for (uint256 i=0; i< auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i ++) {
            if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == highestBidder && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid == highestBid && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                IERC721(gencore).safeTransferFrom(ownerOfToken, highestBidder, _tokenid);
                (bool success, ) = payable(owner()).call{value: highestBid}("");
                emit ClaimAuction(owner(), _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
                (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
                emit Refund(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
            } else {}
        }
    }
```

### Synopsis

`auctionDemo.claimAuction` identifies the NFT owner being forced to transfer the token, but the winning bid is paid to `Ownable.owner()` instead of that token owner. Any valid auction settlement can therefore transfer the NFT away while misrouting sale proceeds.

### Technical Details

In `claimAuction`, the contract snapshots the current NFT owner:

```solidity
address ownerOfToken = IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid);
```

That address is the account whose NFT is later transferred to the auction winner. Economically, this address is the seller for the settled auction because the token is transferred from `ownerOfToken` to `highestBidder`.

However, the ETH settlement leg pays the winning bid to `owner()` from `Ownable`, which is the administrative owner of `auctionDemo`, not necessarily the owner of the auctioned NFT. These are distinct concepts:

- `ownerOfToken`: current ERC721 owner of `_tokenid`, whose asset is sold;
- `owner()`: administrative owner/deployer of the auction contract.

As a result, when `ownerOfToken != owner()`, `claimAuction` finalizes the auction by transferring the NFT from the token owner to the winner, while the token owner receives none of the winning bid. The full winning bid is routed to the auction contract owner instead.

This breaks the core auction settlement invariant: the party losing the asset must receive the sale proceeds.

### Verification Checklist

To verify this issue from code and documentation only, check the following:

- Confirm `auctionDemo` inherits `Ownable`, and `owner()` refers to the contract administrator.
- Confirm the assignment of `ownerOfToken` to `IERC721(gencore).ownerOf(_tokenid)` in `claimAuction` obtains the ERC721 owner of `_tokenid`.
- Confirm the NFT transfer uses `ownerOfToken` as the `from` address during auction settlement.
- Confirm the winning bid payment is sent to `payable(owner())`, not to `ownerOfToken`.
- Confirm there is no separate seller/proceeds-recipient mapping that redirects the winning bid to the NFT owner.
- Confirm `claimAuction` can be called by the winner or an authorized admin after auction end, so settlement can occur even when the NFT owner is not the auction contract owner.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The auction has ended.
- `minter.getAuctionStatus(_tokenid) == true`.
- `auctionClaim[_tokenid] == false`.
- There is at least one active valid bid.
- `ownerOfToken != owner()`.
- `auctionDemo` is approved or otherwise authorized to transfer `_tokenid` from `ownerOfToken`.
- A valid caller invokes `claimAuction(_tokenid)`.

### Proof of Concept

1. `auctionDemo.owner()` is `ProtocolOwner`.
2. Alice owns NFT `_tokenid`.
3. Alice’s NFT is auctioned through `auctionDemo`.
4. Alice approves `auctionDemo` to transfer `_tokenid`.
5. Bob places the highest bid of `10 ether`.
6. The auction reaches its end time.
7. Bob calls `claimAuction(_tokenid)`.
8. The function reads:

```solidity
ownerOfToken = Alice
highestBid = 10 ether
highestBidder = Bob
```

9. Settlement transfers the NFT from Alice to Bob.
10. The winning bid is paid to `auctionDemo.owner()`, i.e. `ProtocolOwner`.
11. Alice loses the NFT and receives `0 ether` from the on-chain settlement.

### Remediation

Pay the winning bid to the NFT owner whose token is being transferred. If protocol fees or treasury routing are required, implement an explicit fee split rather than using `Ownable.owner()` as the implicit seller.

```solidity
(bool success, ) = payable(ownerOfToken).call{value: highestBid}("");
emit ClaimAuction(ownerOfToken, _tokenid, success, highestBid);
```

