### Synopsis

`LandManager._farmPlots` uses the landlord-wide mutable `plotMetadata.lastUpdated` timestamp as the reward cutoff for invalid plots. A landlord can update plot metadata after a lock shrink, causing renters to receive rewards for periods where the plot no longer existed.

### Technical Details

When a staked Munchable’s plot no longer fits within the landlord’s available plot count, `_farmPlots` attempts to cap accrual by replacing `block.timestamp` with `plotMetadata[landlord].lastUpdated`.

The problem is that `plotMetadata[landlord].lastUpdated` is not the timestamp when the specific plot first became invalid. It is a landlord-wide timestamp that is overwritten on later plot metadata updates. Therefore, if a landlord reduces locked value and invalidates a plot, then later performs another lock/metadata update while the plot is still invalid, `_farmPlots` will use the later timestamp as the accrual cutoff.

As a result, rewards are calculated for time during which the renter’s Munchable was assigned to a plot that the landlord no longer had capacity for. The dirty flag is set only after choosing this incorrect timestamp, so the excessive reward is already crystallized and credited to both renter and landlord.

This breaks the core plot lifecycle invariant: schnibbles should accrue only while the landlord has enough locked value to support the occupied plot.

### Verification Checklist

