[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
function computeNAV(address vault, address underlyingToken, bytes calldata managerInput) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (ManagerPrices[] memory managerPrices, PoolInfo[] memory pools, TokenId[][] memory tokenIds) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));

    // vaultPools[vault] check elided

    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        // Oracle price deviation checks elided

        int256 poolExposure0;
        int256 poolExposure1;

        // Signed exposures from premiums (short - long, can be negative):
        // LeftRightUnsigned shortPremium, longPremium;
        // (shortPremium, longPremium, ...) = pools[i].pool.getAccumulatedFeesAndPositionsData(vault, true, tokenIds[i]);
        // poolExposure0 = int256(uint256(shortPremium.rightSlot())) - int256(uint256(longPremium.rightSlot()));
        // poolExposure1 = int256(uint256(longPremium.leftSlot())) - int256(uint256(shortPremium.leftSlot()));
        // + position liquidity deltas (+ short legs, - long legs)
        // + exercised amounts
        // + token0/1 balances (!skipToken)
        // + collateralToken previewRedeem

        // Convert exposures to underlyingToken using oracle TWAP if needed

        // ROOT CAUSE (HL:148): per-pool zero floor before aggregating
        nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0));
    }

    // Direct underlying balance added to nav AFTER per-pool clipping (HL:154)
    // skipUnderlying check elided (underlyingTokens array collects pool tokens)
    nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(vault);
}
```

### Synopsis

`PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` floors each pool’s signed exposure at zero before aggregating portfolio NAV. Offset negative and positive pool exposures are therefore mis-netted, allowing withdrawals or share pricing against overstated NAV and transferring losses to remaining vault users.

### Technical Details

`computeNAV` calculates `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` as signed values for each pool, converts non-underlying exposures to the vault underlying token, and then aggregates the pool into `nav`.

The root cause is the statement inside the loop over pools:

```solidity
nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0));
```

This applies a zero floor per pool inside the loop. As a result, any pool with negative net exposure contributes `0` to NAV instead of reducing the total portfolio value.

The current accounting formula is effectively:

```text
reported NAV = Σ max(poolExposure[i], 0) + unlistedUnderlyingBalance
```

The correct portfolio-level formula should be:

```text
correct NAV = max(Σ poolExposure[i] + unlistedUnderlyingBalance, 0)
```

This difference is material whenever the vault has offsetting positive and negative exposures across pools. For example:

```text
Pool A exposure = -800 underlying
Pool B exposure = +1,000 underlying

Current NAV  = max(-800, 0) + max(1,000, 0) = 1,000
Correct NAV  = max(-800 + 1,000, 0)         = 200
Overstatement = 800
```

Because the vault represents one aggregate portfolio, negative exposures must be netted against positive exposures before applying a final non-negative floor required by the unsigned return type. Applying the floor per pool omits portfolio liabilities and overvalues shares.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify the issue statically by checking:

- `computeNAV` aggregates NAV inside the pool loop with the statement `nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0))`.
- `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` are signed `int256` values and can become negative through long position liabilities, premiums, exercised amounts, or converted token exposures.
- There is no signed portfolio-wide accumulator that preserves negative pool exposure across loop iterations.
- Negative exposure from one pool is discarded before the next pool is processed.
- The final direct underlying balance added after the loop with `nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault)` (if not skipped) also cannot offset discarded pool liabilities.
- Downstream vault accounting uses `computeNAV` as the source of portfolio value for share pricing, deposit fulfillment, withdrawal fulfillment, or redemption calculations.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The vault is configured with at least two valid Panoptic pools.
- At least one pool has a negative signed net exposure after all per-pool calculations and conversions.
- At least one other pool or direct vault balance has positive value.
- The negative exposure represents an economic loss or liability attributable to the vault’s aggregate portfolio.
- An attacker owns or acquires vault shares and can request withdrawal while NAV is overstated.
- The manager/admin acts honestly and processes vault lifecycle operations using the accountant’s returned NAV.

### Proof of Concept

1. Configure a vault with two approved pools in `vaultPools[vault]`.
2. Assume all relevant conversion oracle prices are valid and equal to `1:1` against the vault underlying token.
3. The manager provides valid `managerInput` with correct `PoolInfo[]`, `ManagerPrices[]`, and `TokenId[][]`.
4. Pool A computes to a signed exposure of `-800e18` underlying after calculations from premium accumulation (`getAccumulatedFeesAndPositionsData`) through liquidity amounts, exercised amounts, token balances, collateral (`previewRedeem`), and token conversions (`convert0to1`/`convert1to0`).
5. Pool B computes to a signed exposure of `+1,000e18` underlying after the same sequence of calculations.
6. With the `nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0))` statement inside the loop, Pool A contributes:

```text
max(-800e18, 0) = 0
```

7. With the same statement for Pool B:

```text
max(1,000e18, 0) = 1,000e18
```

8. `computeNAV` returns `1,000e18`.
9. Correct aggregate NAV should be:

```text
max(-800e18 + 1,000e18, 0) = 200e18
```

10. If total vault shares are `100e18` and an attacker owns `50e18`, the inflated NAV prices the attacker’s withdrawal at `500e18` underlying instead of the correct `100e18`.
11. The attacker receives excess underlying from the positive pool’s assets, while the negative exposure remains with the vault and is borne by remaining users.

### Remediation

Accumulate all pool exposures in a signed portfolio-level variable, add any unlisted underlying balance to that same signed accumulator, and apply `Math.max(..., 0)` only once at the end.

```solidity
int256 portfolioExposure; address[] memory underlyingTokens = new address[](pools.length * 2);
portfolioExposure += poolExposure0 + poolExposure1;
if (!skipUnderlying) portfolioExposure += Math.toInt256(IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault));
nav = uint256(Math.max(portfolioExposure, 0));
```

## Signed Panoptic option payoffs are netted across unrelated legs before applying non-recourse valuation in `computeNAV` of `PanopticVaultAccountant`

[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
function computeNAV(address vault, address underlyingToken, bytes calldata managerInput) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (ManagerPrices[] memory managerPrices, PoolInfo[] memory pools, TokenId[][] memory tokenIds) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));
    // ...
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        // price checks
        // ...
        uint256[2][] memory positionBalanceArray;
        int256 poolExposure0;
        int256 poolExposure1;
        {
            LeftRightUnsigned shortPremium;
            LeftRightUnsigned longPremium;
            (shortPremium, longPremium, positionBalanceArray) = pools[i].pool.getAccumulatedFeesAndPositionsData(vault, true, tokenIds[i]);
            poolExposure0 = int256(uint256(shortPremium.rightSlot())) - int256(uint256(longPremium.rightSlot()));
            poolExposure1 = int256(uint256(longPremium.leftSlot())) - int256(uint256(shortPremium.leftSlot()));
        }
        uint256 numLegs;
        for (uint256 j = 0; j < tokenIds[i].length; j++) {
            if (positionBalanceArray[j][1] == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();
            uint256 positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs();
            for (uint256 k = 0; k < positionLegs; k++) {
                (uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) = Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(
                    managerPrices[i].poolPrice,
                    PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk(tokenIds[i][j], k, uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]))
                );
                if (tokenIds[i][j].isLong(k) == 0) {
                    poolExposure0 += int256(amount0);
                    poolExposure1 += int256(amount1);
                } else {
                    poolExposure0 -= int256(amount0);
                    poolExposure1 -= int256(amount1);
                }
            }
            (LeftRightSigned longAmounts, LeftRightSigned shortAmounts) = PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts(tokenIds[i][j], uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]));
            poolExposure0 += int256(longAmounts.rightSlot()) - int256(shortAmounts.rightSlot());
            poolExposure1 += int256(longAmounts.leftSlot()) - int256(shortAmounts.leftSlot());
            numLegs += positionLegs;
        }
        if (numLegs != pools[i].pool.numberOfLegs(vault)) revert IncorrectPositionList();
        // balances, collateral, conversions...
        // ...
        nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0));
    }
}
```

### Synopsis

`PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` aggregates signed liquidity and exercised amounts from all Panoptic legs into shared pool-level exposure variables. Negative intrinsic value from one position can offset positive value from another, causing materially understated or distorted vault NAV and unfair share pricing.

### Technical Details

The highlighted block manually reconstructs Panoptic position value by combining two components:

- current liquidity value for each leg, added for short legs and subtracted for long legs;
- exercised/notional amounts from `PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts`, added as `longAmounts - shortAmounts`.

The issue is that these signed values are written directly into the shared `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` accumulators:

- short liquidity is added in the statements adding liquidity amounts for short legs to `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` (inside the `if (tokenIds[i][j].isLong(k) == 0)` branch of the inner per-leg loop);
- long liquidity is subtracted in the statements subtracting liquidity amounts for long legs from `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` (inside the `else` branch of the inner per-leg loop);
- aggregate exercised amounts are added/subtracted in the statements adjusting `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` by `longAmounts - shortAmounts` returned from `PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts` (immediately after the per-leg loop, for each position).

This means the accountant first computes a single net signed exposure for the entire Panoptic pool. A profitable position and an underwater position in the same pool are allowed to cancel each other before the portfolio value is finalized.

That diverges from Panoptic option settlement semantics where the economic value of a long/non-recourse option position cannot be less than zero beyond the collateral/premium already committed to that position. Underwater optionality should not reduce the realizable value of unrelated profitable optionality. The accounting logic therefore converts optional, position-level claims into cross-netted pool-level debt.

As a result, the NAV can be understated when negative signed exposure from an out-of-the-money or abandoned position offsets positive signed exposure from an in-the-money position. Users can exploit the understated NAV by depositing at an artificially low share price before the profitable position is realized or before the negative position is abandoned/settled.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify this issue statically by checking the following:

- Confirm that `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` are shared accumulators for the whole pool, not local per-position or per-leg valuation variables.
- Confirm that the statements adding liquidity amounts for short legs to `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` (inside the `if (tokenIds[i][j].isLong(k) == 0)` branch of the inner per-leg loop) add short-leg liquidity directly into those shared accumulators.
- Confirm that the statements subtracting liquidity amounts for long legs from `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` (inside the `else` branch of the inner per-leg loop) subtract long-leg liquidity directly from those same shared accumulators.
- Confirm that the statements adjusting `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` by `longAmounts - shortAmounts` returned from `PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts` (immediately after the per-leg loop, for each position) add aggregate exercised amounts directly into the same shared accumulators.
- Confirm that there is no per-position settlement, payoff floor, or canonical Panoptic account-value calculation before adding a position’s signed value to the pool exposure.
- Confirm, using Panoptic’s position settlement documentation/code, that underwater optionality should not be allowed to consume unrelated profitable optionality before NAV is computed.
- Confirm that later NAV calculation uses the already-netted pool exposure, so the loss of position-level granularity cannot be recovered afterward.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The vault holds at least two Panoptic positions or legs in the same pool.
- At least one position has positive signed intrinsic value under the accountant’s formula.
- At least one unrelated position has negative signed intrinsic value under the same formula.
- Vault shares are minted/redeemed using the NAV returned by `computeNAV`.
- A user can deposit or withdraw around the period where the incorrect NAV is used.
- The manager and owner are honest; exploitation relies on deterministic misvaluation, not privileged abuse.

### Proof of Concept

1. Assume the vault holds two independent long Panoptic positions in the same pool.
2. Position A is in-the-money:
   - liquidity component: `-100`;
   - exercised component: `+200`;
   - true realizable intrinsic value: `+100`.
3. Position B is out-of-the-money:
   - liquidity component: `-200`;
   - exercised component: `+100`;
   - signed accountant value: `-100`;
   - realizable optional value should be `0`, not `-100`.
4. During `computeNAV`, both positions are accumulated into the same `poolExposure0/poolExposure1` variables.
5. The accountant computes:
   - Position A contribution: `+100`;
   - Position B contribution: `-100`;
   - net pool exposure: `0`.
6. The vault NAV excludes the `+100` realizable value of Position A because it was cancelled by Position B’s negative signed value.
7. An attacker deposits when the NAV is understated.
8. Later, the manager realizes Position A and lets Position B expire, be abandoned, or otherwise settle without realizing the artificial negative value.
9. NAV increases, and the attacker receives excess vault value at the expense of existing shareholders.

### Remediation

Do not aggregate signed Panoptic leg values directly into pool-level exposure before applying Panoptic’s canonical settlement semantics.

Preferred fix:

- compute each position’s value independently;
- convert the position’s token0/token1 exposure into the vault underlying;
- apply the correct Panoptic non-recourse/settlement floor at the correct granularity;
- only then add the finalized non-negative realizable position value to NAV.

Alternatively, replace the manual liquidity/exercise reconstruction with an official Panoptic account-valuation helper that returns the canonical realizable account value.

The affected logic is the highlighted valuation block encompassing the per-position loop that iterates over each position's legs to compute liquidity chunks via `PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk` and `Math.getAmountsForLiquidity` at `managerPrices[i].poolPrice`, accumulates signed amounts into shared `poolExposure0`/`poolExposure1`, and adjusts for exercised amounts via `PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts` (from `positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs()` to `numLegs += positionLegs`); specifically, the statements adding liquidity amounts for short legs to `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` (inside the `if (tokenIds[i][j].isLong(k) == 0)` branch), the statements subtracting liquidity amounts for long legs from `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` (inside the `else` branch), and the statements adjusting `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` by `longAmounts - shortAmounts` must not write raw signed per-leg/per-position values directly into the shared pool exposure accumulators.

## Packed `PositionBalance` validation accepts zero-size stale positions in `computeNAV` of `PanopticVaultAccountant`

[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
/// @notice Returns the NAV of the portfolio contained in `vault` in terms of its underlying token.
function computeNAV(
    address vault,
    address underlyingToken,
    bytes calldata managerInput
) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (ManagerPrices[] memory managerPrices, PoolInfo[] memory pools, TokenId[][] memory tokenIds) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));
    address _vault = vault;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        uint256[2][] memory positionBalanceArray;
        LeftRightUnsigned shortPremium;
        LeftRightUnsigned longPremium;
        (shortPremium, longPremium, positionBalanceArray) = pools[i].pool.getAccumulatedFeesAndPositionsData(_vault, true, tokenIds[i]);
        uint256 numLegs;
        for (uint256 j = 0; j < tokenIds[i].length; j++) {
            if (positionBalanceArray[j][1] == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();
            uint256 positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs();
            for (uint256 k = 0; k < positionLegs; k++) {
                Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(
                    managerPrices[i].poolPrice,
                    PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk(tokenIds[i][j], k, uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]))
                );
// ... exposure calculations using uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]) ...
            }
            (LeftRightSigned longAmounts, LeftRightSigned shortAmounts) = PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts(tokenIds[i][j], uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]));
// ... add to exposure ...
            numLegs += positionLegs;
        }
        if (numLegs != pools[i].pool.numberOfLegs(_vault)) revert IncorrectPositionList();
// ... rest of pool processing, nav accumulation ...
    }
