for (uint256 m = 0; m < j; ++m) {
if (TokenId.unwrap(tokenIds[i][m]) == TokenId.unwrap(tokenIds[i][j]))
revert IncorrectPositionList();
}
uint128 positionSize = uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]);
if (positionSize == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();
uint256 positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs();
for (uint256 k = 0; k < positionLegs; k++) {
(uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) = Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(
managerPrices[i].poolPrice,
PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk(tokenIds[i][j], k, positionSize)
);
(LeftRightSigned longAmounts, LeftRightSigned shortAmounts) = PanopticMath
.computeExercisedAmounts(tokenIds[i][j], positionSize);
if (numLegs != pools[i].pool.numberOfLegs(_vault)) revert IncorrectPositionList();
```

## Raw vault underlying balance is counted as NAV in `computeNAV` of `PanopticVaultAccountant`

[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
/// @notice Returns the NAV of the portfolio contained in `vault` in terms of its underlying token.
/// @param vault The address of the vault to value
/// @param underlyingToken The underlying token of the vault
/// @param managerInput Input calldata from the vault manager...
/// @return nav The NAV of the portfolio...
function computeNAV(
    address vault,
    address underlyingToken,
    bytes calldata managerInput
) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (ManagerPrices[] memory managerPrices, PoolInfo[] memory pools, TokenId[][] memory tokenIds) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));
    address[] memory underlyingTokens = new address[](pools.length * 2);
    address _vault = vault;
    // For each pool: compute exposures from positions/fees/collateral, add pool token balances (!skipToken), convert to underlying, nav += max(exposure0 + exposure1, 0)
    // underlyingTokens filled with *unique* pool.token0 and pool.token1
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        bool skipToken0 = false; bool skipToken1 = false;
        for (uint256 j = 0; j < underlyingTokens.length; j++) {
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(pools[i].token0)) skipToken0 = true;
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(pools[i].token1)) skipToken1 = true;
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(0)) {
                if (!skipToken0) underlyingTokens[j] = address(pools[i].token0);
                if (!skipToken1) underlyingTokens[j + (skipToken0 ? 0 : 1)] = address(pools[i].token1);
                break;
            }
        }
        // if (!skipToken0) poolExposure0 += token0.balanceOf(_vault)
        // if (!skipToken1) poolExposure1 += token1.balanceOf(_vault)
        // ... nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0));
    }
    // Vulnerability: raw balance added if underlyingToken not a pool token
    bool skipUnderlying = false;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < underlyingTokens.length; i++) {
        if (underlyingTokens[i] == underlyingToken) skipUnderlying = true;
    }
    if (!skipUnderlying) nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault);
}
```

### Synopsis

`PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` adds the vault’s raw `underlyingToken.balanceOf(vault)` to NAV. For asynchronous vaults, this balance can include queued deposits or other user-owned liabilities not yet represented by shares, inflating NAV and enabling overpayment of withdrawals.

### Technical Details

The highlighted final aggregation block determines whether the vault’s direct underlying balance was already included through listed pool tokens:

```solidity
bool skipUnderlying = false;
for (uint256 i = 0; i < underlyingTokens.length; i++) {
if (underlyingTokens[i] == underlyingToken) skipUnderlying = true;
}
if (!skipUnderlying) nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault);
```

The root issue is the statement `if (!skipUnderlying) nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault);`: it treats the entire raw ERC20 balance held by the vault as NAV belonging to the current share supply.

In the documented vault architecture, deposits are asynchronous: users transfer `underlyingToken` into the vault when requesting a deposit, while shares are minted only later after the deposit epoch is fulfilled/executed. During this pending interval, the vault’s ERC20 balance includes assets that economically belong to pending depositors and are not yet represented by minted vault shares.

Because `computeNAV` has no liability-aware adjustment, pending deposit assets are included in NAV. Any share pricing operation using this NAV before those deposits are converted into shares can overstate the share price of existing shareholders. A withdrawing shareholder can be paid from assets that should belong to pending depositors.

This is not merely a display issue: NAV is the basis for share-to-asset conversion in vault lifecycle operations. Counting raw balance instead of accountable balance breaks the invariant that NAV must represent assets backing the currently issued shares.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer should verify the following in the codebase:

- `computeNAV` adds `IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault)` directly in the `if (!skipUnderlying)` block.
- No highlighted logic subtracts pending deposits, unminted deposit liabilities, withdrawal reserves, or other user-owned underlying balances.
- The vault deposit flow transfers `underlyingToken` into the vault before corresponding shares are minted.
- Pending deposit assets remain at the same `_vault` address read by `balanceOf(_vault)`.
- NAV returned by `computeNAV` is used to price deposits, withdrawals, or share conversions.
- There is no separate escrow contract or accounting-only vault balance used by the accountant.
- The issue is reachable when `underlyingToken` is not present in the registered pool token list, causing `skipUnderlying == false` and executing the `nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault)` statement.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The vault uses asynchronous deposits where assets can be held by the vault before shares are minted.
- `underlyingToken` is not one of the pool tokens recorded in `underlyingTokens`, so line `HL:154` executes.
- A withdrawal or other NAV-priced operation can be fulfilled while pending deposit assets are held by the vault.
- The manager/owner acts honestly; exploitation only requires normal vault lifecycle timing and user interaction.
- Pending deposit assets are not otherwise subtracted from NAV before or after `computeNAV`.

### Proof of Concept

1. Assume a vault has:
   - `1,000 USDC` of true managed assets backing existing shares.
   - `1,000` outstanding shares.
   - Correct share price: `1 USDC/share`.

2. An attacker owns `100` shares and requests withdrawal.

3. Before the withdrawal is fulfilled, another user queues a deposit of `9,000 USDC`.

4. The queued deposit transfers `9,000 USDC` into the vault, but no shares have been minted to the depositor yet.

5. `computeNAV` is called while the queued deposit is still pending.

6. Since `underlyingToken` is not in `underlyingTokens`, `skipUnderlying` remains `false`.

7. The `if (!skipUnderlying) nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault)` statement adds the full raw vault USDC balance:

```text
reported NAV = 1,000 managed assets + 9,000 pending deposit assets
             = 10,000 USDC
```

8. The withdrawal is priced at:

```text
10,000 USDC / 1,000 shares = 10 USDC/share
```

9. The attacker’s `100` shares are redeemed for:

```text
100 * 10 = 1,000 USDC
```

10. Correct payout should have been:

```text
100 * 1 = 100 USDC
```

11. The attacker extracts `900 USDC` of excess value from assets that should have backed the pending depositor or remaining vault participants.

### Remediation

Use a liability-aware accountable balance instead of the raw ERC20 balance. The vault should expose or provide an accounting value that excludes pending deposits, unminted assets, reserved withdrawal assets, and any other balances not backing the current share supply.

Preferred fix: replace the raw balance read with a vault-provided accountable underlying balance.

```solidity
if (!skipUnderlying) nav += IAccountingVault(_vault).accountableBalanceOf(underlyingToken);
```

The `accountableBalanceOf()` implementation should return only the portion of the vault’s direct underlying balance that economically belongs to current vault shareholders.

## Collateral tracker share balances can be double-counted as pool-token wallet balances in `computeNAV` of `PanopticVaultAccountant`

[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
function computeNAV(address vault, address underlyingToken, bytes calldata managerInput) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (, PoolInfo[] memory pools, ) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));
    if (keccak256(abi.encode(pools)) != vaultPools[vault]) revert InvalidPools();
    address[] memory underlyingTokens = new address[](pools.length * 2);
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        int256 poolExposure0 = 0, poolExposure1 = 0;
        bool skipToken0 = false, skipToken1 = false;
        // Deduplication only for pools[i].token0/token1 (not collateralToken0/1)
        for (uint256 j = 0; j < underlyingTokens.length; j++) {
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(pools[i].token0)) skipToken0 = true;
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(pools[i].token1)) skipToken1 = true;
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(0)) {
                if (!skipToken0) underlyingTokens[j] = address(pools[i].token0);
                if (!skipToken1) underlyingTokens[j + (skipToken0 ? 0 : 1)] = address(pools[i].token1);
                break;
            }
        }
        // HL110-111: Raw pool-token balances
        if (!skipToken0) poolExposure0 += int256(pools[i].token0.balanceOf(vault));
        if (!skipToken1) poolExposure1 += int256(pools[i].token1.balanceOf(vault));
        // HL112-119: Collateral tracker shares (double-counted if pool token elsewhere)
        uint256 collateralBalance = pools[i].pool.collateralToken0().balanceOf(vault);
        poolExposure0 += int256(pools[i].pool.collateralToken0().previewRedeem(collateralBalance));
        collateralBalance = pools[i].pool.collateralToken1().balanceOf(vault);
        poolExposure1 += int256(pools[i].pool.collateralToken1().previewRedeem(collateralBalance));
        // Omitted: HL120-146 conversions to underlying
        nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0));
    }
}
```

### Synopsis

`PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` can count the same collateral tracker shares twice: once via `previewRedeem()` as Panoptic collateral, and again as a raw pool-token wallet balance when that collateral token is listed as another pool’s token, inflating NAV and share value.

### Technical Details

The highlighted logic values vault assets through two independent paths:

1. Raw pool-token wallet balances:
   - `pools[i].token0.balanceOf(_vault)` in the conditional addition to `poolExposure0` when `!skipToken0`
   - `pools[i].token1.balanceOf(_vault)` in the conditional addition to `poolExposure1` when `!skipToken1`

2. Panoptic collateral tracker balances:
   - `pools[i].pool.collateralToken0().balanceOf(_vault)` followed by `previewRedeem()` in the block adding to `poolExposure0`
   - `pools[i].pool.collateralToken1().balanceOf(_vault)` followed by `previewRedeem()` in the block adding to `poolExposure1`

The code deduplicates raw pool-token balances using `underlyingTokens`, but that deduplication only tracks `pools[i].token0` and `pools[i].token1`. It does not track or exclude `collateralToken0()` / `collateralToken1()` addresses.

Because `CollateralTracker` is an ERC20-like receipt/share token, it can itself appear as `token0` or `token1` in another registered pool. In that case, the same vault-held collateral tracker shares are:

- valued once as redeemable collateral via `previewRedeem()`;
- valued again as a direct wallet balance of the pool token;
- then converted to the vault underlying through the `if (address(pools[i].token0) != underlyingToken)` block or the `if (address(pools[i].token1) != underlyingToken)` block.

This creates an inflated NAV without requiring a hash bypass or invalid calldata. The pool list can match the approved `vaultPools[vault]` hash, while still containing overlapping asset identities that the accountant does not reject or deduplicate.

### Verification Checklist

A security engineer can verify the issue statically by checking:

- The raw pool token balance additions to `poolExposure0` and `poolExposure1` using `pools[i].token0.balanceOf(_vault)` and `pools[i].token1.balanceOf(_vault)` when not skipped.
- The separate additions of the vault’s balances of `collateralToken0()` and `collateralToken1()` using `previewRedeem()` in their respective blocks.
- The token deduplication array used before the raw pool token balance additions only stores pool token addresses, not collateral tracker token addresses.
- There is no check in the highlighted logic preventing:
  - `address(pools[x].token0) == address(pools[y].pool.collateralToken0())`;
  - `address(pools[x].token1) == address(pools[y].pool.collateralToken0())`;
  - `address(pools[x].token0) == address(pools[y].pool.collateralToken1())`;
  - `address(pools[x].token1) == address(pools[y].pool.collateralToken1())`.
- There is no later correction subtracting one of the two counts.
- The exposure conversion blocks can convert the second raw-balance count into the vault underlying, making the double count affect returned NAV.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The approved `PoolInfo[]` contains at least two pools.
- A collateral tracker token from one registered Panoptic pool is also used as `token0` or `token1` in another registered pool.
- The vault holds a positive balance of that collateral tracker token.
- The relevant oracle configuration allows the collateral tracker token balance to be converted to the vault underlying.
- The returned NAV is used by the vault for share pricing, withdrawals, deposits, or fee/accounting decisions.

### Proof of Concept

1. Assume the vault underlying token is USDC.

2. Pool A is a registered Panoptic pool whose `collateralToken0()` is `ctUSDC`.

3. The vault owns `1,000 ctUSDC` shares.

4. `ctUSDC.previewRedeem(1,000 ctUSDC)` returns `1,000 USDC`.

5. Pool B is also registered and uses `ctUSDC` as one of its pool tokens, for example:

```text
pools[B].token0 = ctUSDC
pools[B].token1 = USDC
```

6. During NAV computation for Pool A:

```text
collateralBalance = ctUSDC.balanceOf(vault) = 1,000
previewRedeem(collateralBalance) = 1,000 USDC
```

   The `collateralToken0` `previewRedeem` block adds `1,000 USDC` of value to `poolExposure0`.

7. During NAV computation for Pool B, `ctUSDC` is treated as a raw pool token:

```text
pools[B].token0.balanceOf(vault) = ctUSDC.balanceOf(vault) = 1,000
```

   The conditional `token0` balance addition adds the same `1,000 ctUSDC` balance again to `poolExposure0`.

8. The `if (address(pools[i].token0) != underlyingToken)` conversion block then convert that `ctUSDC` exposure into the vault underlying, adding approximately another `1,000 USDC`.

9. The accountant reports approximately:

```text
reported NAV = 2,000 USDC
actual economic value = 1,000 USDC
```

10. A withdrawing user can receive assets based on the inflated NAV, extracting more underlying than their shares are economically worth and leaving the shortfall to remaining vault users.

### Remediation

Ensure each vault-held asset address is valued through exactly one path. The preferred fix is to skip raw wallet-balance inclusion for any token address that is also a registered Panoptic collateral tracker token, and value such tokens only through `previewRedeem()`.

Alternatively, reject any `PoolInfo[]` where a pool token address overlaps with any registered pool’s `collateralToken0()` or `collateralToken1()`.

Example corrected highlighted lines using a helper such as `_isRegisteredCollateralToken(pools, token)`:

```solidity
: (_isRegisteredCollateralToken(pools, address(pools[i].token0)) ? int256(0) : int256(pools[i].token0.balanceOf(_vault)));
if (!skipToken1 && !_isRegisteredCollateralToken(pools, address(pools[i].token1))) poolExposure1 += int256(pools[i].token1.balanceOf(_vault));
```

The helper should return `true` if `token` equals any `address(pools[j].pool.collateralToken0())` or `address(pools[j].pool.collateralToken1())` for the registered pool list.

## Order-dependent oracle selection for shared vault token balances in `computeNAV` of `PanopticVaultAccountant`

[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
function computeNAV(address vault, address underlyingToken, bytes calldata managerInput) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (ManagerPrices[] memory managerPrices, PoolInfo[] memory pools, TokenId[][] memory tokenIds) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));
    if (keccak256(abi.encode(pools)) != vaultPools[vault]) revert InvalidPools();
    address[] memory underlyingTokens = new address[](pools.length * 2);
    address _vault = vault;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        // Omitted: pool price check, position fees/legs/exposure, collateral
        if (address(pools[i].token0) == address(0)) pools[i].token0 = IERC20Partial(0xEeeeeEeeeEeEeeEeEeEeeEEEeeeeEeeeeeeeEEeE);
        bool skipToken0 = false, skipToken1 = false;
        for (uint256 j = 0; j < underlyingTokens.length; j++) {
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(pools[i].token0)) skipToken0 = true;
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(pools[i].token1)) skipToken1 = true;
            if (underlyingTokens[j] == address(0)) {
                if (!skipToken0) underlyingTokens[j] = address(pools[i].token0);
                if (!skipToken1) underlyingTokens[j + (skipToken0 ? 0 : 1)] = address(pools[i].token1);
                break;
            }
        }
        int256 poolExposure0 = 0;
        int256 poolExposure1 = 0;
        if (!skipToken0) {
            address t0 = address(pools[i].token0);
            poolExposure0 += t0 == 0xEeeeeEeeeEeEeeEeEeEeeEEEeeeeEeeeeeeeEEeE ? int256(_vault.balance) : int256(pools[i].token0.balanceOf(_vault));
        }
        if (!skipToken1) poolExposure1 += int256(pools[i].token1.balanceOf(_vault));
        // Omitted: oracle/TWAP/deviation checks
        // Convert using pools[i].oracle0/oracle1 etc. to underlyingToken
        // e.g. poolExposure0 = PanopticMath.convert0to1(poolExposure0, conversionPrice);
        nav += uint256(Math.max(poolExposure0 + poolExposure1, 0));
    }
    // Omitted: underlyingToken balance addition if not seen
}
```

### Synopsis

`PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` de-duplicates vault-held token balances by token address only. When the same token appears in multiple pools, the first pool in the approved order determines the conversion oracle for the vault-wide balance, enabling incorrect or manipulable NAV valuation.

### Technical Details

The highlighted de-duplication logic uses `underlyingTokens` as a simple list of token addresses already encountered:

- the loop `for (uint256 j = 0; j < underlyingTokens.length; j++)` that checks `underlyingTokens[j]` against `address(pools[i].token0)` and `address(pools[i].token1)` to set `skipToken0` and `skipToken1` scans for previously seen token addresses;
- the assignments `underlyingTokens[j] = address(pools[i].token0)` and `underlyingTokens[j + (skipToken0 ? 0 : 1)] = address(pools[i].token1)` when `underlyingTokens[j] == address(0)` record the first occurrence of `pools[i].token0` / `pools[i].token1`;
- the conditional `if (!skipToken0) poolExposure0 += address(pools[i].token0) == address(0xEeeeeEeeeEeEeeEeEeEeeEEEeeeeEeeeeeeeEEeE) ? int256(address(_vault).balance) : int256(pools[i].token0.balanceOf(_vault))` adds the vault’s entire balance of `token0` to the current pool’s exposure when `token0` has not been seen before.

This means a vault-wide token balance is attributed to whichever pool first mentions that token. The code does not also bind the token to the oracle, orientation, TWAP window, or deviation parameters that will later be used to convert that balance into the vault’s `underlyingToken`.

If the same token appears in multiple approved pools with different conversion oracles, the NAV of the entire vault-held balance of that shared token becomes dependent on the order of `pools[]`. Later occurrences of the same token are skipped because `skipToken0` / `skipToken1` are set based only on address equality, so their potentially correct or more robust oracle configuration is ignored.

The pool hash validation only proves that the submitted pool array matches the owner-approved array. It does not prevent this semantic error because the approved array itself can contain multiple pools sharing a token but using different token-to-underlying oracle configurations. As a result, a weak, stale, or manipulated oracle from the first occurrence can price the vault’s full direct balance of that token.

This is distinct from pool-position valuation: the affected balance is a vault-wide wallet balance, but its conversion path is selected by pool order rather than by a canonical per-token valuation rule.

### Verification Checklist

To verify the issue statically, check the following:

1. Confirm that `underlyingTokens` stores only token addresses and no oracle metadata.
2. Confirm that `skipToken0` / `skipToken1` are set only by comparing `underlyingTokens[j]` to `address(pools[i].token0)` and `address(pools[i].token1)`.
3. Confirm that the first occurrence of a token is inserted at the assignments `underlyingTokens[j] = address(pools[i].token0)` and `underlyingTokens[j + (skipToken0 ? 0 : 1)] = address(pools[i].token1)` when `underlyingTokens[j] == address(0)`.
4. Confirm that the vault-wide token balance is added only when the token is not skipped.
5. Confirm that later conversion to `underlyingToken` uses the current pool’s oracle configuration, not a canonical per-token oracle.
6. Confirm that there is no validation requiring all occurrences of the same token across `pools[]` to use the same oracle, orientation, TWAP window, and deviation settings.
7. Confirm that `vaultPools[vault]` validates only the hash of the array and does not normalize or validate shared-token oracle consistency.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The vault uses `PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` to price deposits, withdrawals, or share accounting.
- The approved pool list contains at least two pools sharing the same non-underlying token.
- The shared token has different conversion oracle configuration across those pool entries, or one of the configured oracles is weaker, stale, or easier to manipulate.
- The vault directly holds a balance of that shared token.
- The attacker can benefit from NAV mispricing, for example by depositing when NAV is understated or withdrawing/redeeming when NAV is overstated.
- The owner/admin is honest; the issue arises from order-dependent accounting semantics, not from malicious administrative action.

### Proof of Concept

1. Assume the vault’s `underlyingToken` is USDC.
2. The approved `pools[]` array contains two pools in this order:
   - `pools[0].token0 = WETH`, with `pools[0].oracle0 = OracleA`;
   - `pools[1].token1 = WETH`, with `pools[1].oracle1 = OracleB`.
3. `OracleA` and `OracleB` are both approved as part of the pool hash, but `OracleA` is less liquid or temporarily manipulable.
4. The vault directly holds `1,000 WETH`.
5. During `computeNAV`, the loop reaches `pools[0]`.
6. `underlyingTokens` is empty, so `skipToken0 == false`.
7. The assignment `underlyingTokens[j] = address(pools[i].token0)` when `underlyingTokens[j] == address(0)` records WETH as already seen.
8. The conditional `if (!skipToken0) poolExposure0 += ...` causes the vault’s full WETH wallet balance to be included through the `pools[0]` accounting path.
9. The later conversion of that WETH exposure uses `pools[0].oracle0`, i.e. `OracleA`.
10. When the loop reaches `pools[1]`, WETH is already present in `underlyingTokens`, so the WETH balance is skipped and `OracleB` is never used for that direct WETH balance.
11. If `OracleA` is manipulated downward, NAV is understated and an attacker can deposit for excess shares.
12. When `OracleA` normalizes, the attacker’s shares represent more vault assets than they paid for.
13. Conversely, if `OracleA` is manipulated upward, an existing shareholder can withdraw or redeem against an overstated NAV.

### Remediation

Do not use first-seen pool order to determine how vault-wide token balances are valued.

Preferred fix:

- Separate pool-position valuation from direct wallet-balance valuation.
- Build a canonical per-token valuation configuration for every non-underlying token held by the vault.
- Add each vault-wide token balance to NAV exactly once in a separate pass.
- Convert that balance using the canonical token-to-underlying oracle for that token.
- Reject pool configurations where the same token appears with conflicting oracle, orientation, TWAP window, or deviation settings unless an explicit canonical oracle is configured.

If retaining the current pool-based configuration, add validation that every repeated token address across `pools[]` has identical conversion parameters before using the first occurrence.

Do not allow the highlighted de-duplication logic (the `underlyingTokens` scanning loop, skip flag settings, token address insertions, and conditional vault balance additions to `poolExposure0`/`poolExposure1`) to silently select the valuation oracle based only on pool order.

## Zero-balance Panoptic positions can be counted as active legs in `computeNAV` of `PanopticVaultAccountant`

[src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259](https://github.com/evmbench-org/2025-06-panoptic/blob/eea2c931b1cbce1da01586e42ba298814de40d31/src/accountants/PanopticVaultAccountant.sol#L89-L259)

```solidity
function computeNAV(address vault, address underlyingToken, bytes calldata managerInput) external view returns (uint256 nav) {
    (ManagerPrices[] memory managerPrices, PoolInfo[] memory pools, TokenId[][] memory tokenIds) = abi.decode(managerInput, (ManagerPrices[], PoolInfo[], TokenId[][]));
    if (keccak256(abi.encode(pools)) != vaultPools[vault]) revert InvalidPools();
    address _vault = vault;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < pools.length; i++) {
        uint256[2][] memory positionBalanceArray;
        {
            LeftRightUnsigned shortPremium;
            LeftRightUnsigned longPremium;
            (shortPremium, longPremium, positionBalanceArray) = pools[i].pool.getAccumulatedFeesAndPositionsData(_vault, true, tokenIds[i]);
            // ignore premiums
        }
        uint256 numLegs;
        for (uint256 j = 0; j < tokenIds[i].length; j++) {
            if (positionBalanceArray[j][1] == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();
            uint256 positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs();
            for (uint256 k = 0; k < positionLegs; k++) {
                Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(
                    managerPrices[i].poolPrice,
                    PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk(tokenIds[i][j], k, uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]))
                );
                // exposure += amounts (zero if uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]) == 0)
            }
            PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts(tokenIds[i][j], uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]));
            // exposure += exercised (zero if uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]) == 0)
            numLegs += positionLegs;
        }
        if (numLegs != pools[i].pool.numberOfLegs(_vault)) revert IncorrectPositionList();
        // remaining NAV logic omitted
    }
}
```

### Synopsis

`PanopticVaultAccountant.computeNAV` counts legs from supplied `tokenIds` before validating that the extracted low-128-bit position size is non-zero. Metadata-only Panoptic `PositionBalance` words can therefore contribute legs while adding zero exposure, allowing active option liabilities to be omitted from NAV.

### Technical Details

In the loop over positions within each pool (immediately after the `if (positionBalanceArray[j][1] == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();` check), the accountant derives the number of legs from the manager-supplied token ID:

```solidity
uint256 positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs();
```

It later values the position using:

```solidity
uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1])
uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1])
```

`positionBalanceArray[j][1]` is a packed Panoptic `PositionBalance`, containing the actual position balance plus additional metadata such as utilization/oracle tick data. The logic extracts the low 128 bits as the actual position size only at valuation time, but it never checks that this extracted `uint128` balance is non-zero before counting the token ID’s legs.

As a result, a packed `PositionBalance` with:

- low 128-bit balance equal to `0`, and
- non-zero metadata bits,

is valued with position size `0`, so:

- `Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(...)` contributes zero liquidity exposure;
- `PanopticMath.computeExercisedAmounts(..., 0)` contributes zero exercised exposure;
- but `numLegs += positionLegs` still counts the supplied token ID as an active position.

This allows a metadata-only or stale zero-balance position to act as a leg-count placeholder. If its leg count matches an omitted active position, the final leg-count check can still pass, while the omitted active position’s exposure is not included in NAV.

### Verification Checklist

Check the following in the codebase and Panoptic documentation:

- Confirm `positionBalanceArray[j][1]` is a packed `PositionBalance`, not a raw position size.
- Confirm the actual position size is obtained from the low 128 bits, equivalent to `uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1])`.
- Confirm the position leg processing code (deriving `positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs()`, the `for (uint256 k = 0; k < positionLegs; k++)` loop calling `getLiquidityChunk` and accumulating exposures, calling `computeExercisedAmounts`, and `numLegs += positionLegs`) following the `if (positionBalanceArray[j][1] == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();` check does not validate that this extracted `uint128` position size is non-zero before:
  - looping over `positionLegs`;
  - calling `getLiquidityChunk`;
  - calling `computeExercisedAmounts`;
  - incrementing `numLegs`.
- Confirm Panoptic can return or retain a non-zero packed `PositionBalance` word whose actual balance is zero but metadata bits are non-zero.
- Confirm `numLegs += positionLegs` counts legs from the supplied token ID, independent of the extracted position size.
- Confirm `Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(..., positionSize = 0)` and `computeExercisedAmounts(..., positionSize = 0)` produce zero exposure.
- Confirm the later pool leg-count validation compares only aggregate leg count, not the identity and non-zero balance of each active position.

### Assumptions and Preconditions

- The caller can influence `managerInput`, specifically the `tokenIds[i]` list used by `computeNAV`.
- At least one supplied token ID has a packed `PositionBalance` with non-zero metadata but zero actual low-128-bit balance.
- The supplied zero-size token IDs can be chosen so their `countLegs()` total matches the pool’s actual active leg count.
- The omitted active position has material positive or negative exposure.
- The registered `PoolInfo[]` hash is valid, so the computation proceeds past pool validation.

### Proof of Concept

1. Assume the vault currently has one active Panoptic position:

```text
tokenIdActive.countLegs() = 2
actual exposure = -1,000 underlying
```

2. Assume the vault also has a stale or metadata-only Panoptic position:

```text
tokenIdClosed.countLegs() = 2
positionBalanceArray[j][1] = non-zero metadata bits | low128 balance = 0
```

3. The attacker supplies the following position list for the pool:

```text
tokenIds[i] = [tokenIdClosed]
```

4. During the position processing loop for the pool (after passing `if (positionBalanceArray[j][1] == 0)` since metadata non-zero):

```solidity
positionLegs = tokenIdClosed.countLegs(); // 2
```

5. The valuation calls use the extracted low-128-bit balance:

```solidity
uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]) // 0
uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]) // 0
```

6. Therefore the liquidity and exercised amount contributions are zero.

7. The leg counter is still incremented:

```solidity
numLegs += positionLegs; // numLegs = 2
```

8. The aggregate leg-count check passes because the real active position also has two legs.

9. The active position’s `-1,000` underlying exposure is omitted from NAV, causing NAV to be overstated by `1,000` underlying.

### Remediation

Extract the actual position size once, require it to be non-zero, and use the validated value for both liquidity and exercised amount calculations.

```solidity
uint128 positionSize = uint128(positionBalanceArray[j][1]);
if (positionSize == 0) revert IncorrectPositionList();
uint256 positionLegs = tokenIds[i][j].countLegs();
for (uint256 k = 0; k < positionLegs; k++) {
(uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) = Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(
managerPrices[i].poolPrice,
PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk(
tokenIds[i][j],
k,
positionSize
)
);
(LeftRightSigned longAmounts, LeftRightSigned shortAmounts) = PanopticMath
.computeExercisedAmounts(tokenIds[i][j], positionSize);
```

